# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "Skip and the consequence rule" (first part).
id: skip-consequence
title: Skip and the consequence rule
topic: Skip and consequence
level: 1
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "Skip and the consequence rule"
tags: skip, consequence, implication

task:
  Using the Skip rule and the consequence rule, show that the following
  triple is valid.

pre: x > 5
post: x > 0

program:
  {P}
  {R1}
  skip        // i1
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of skip
rule: SKIP, WP
focus: i1
text:
  `skip` does not change the state. By the Skip rule, its weakest
  precondition is its postcondition, here the postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(skip, Q)
  = x > 0       // wp(skip, Q) is Q
set: R1 = x > 0
instance: SKIP
  ---
  {x > 0} skip {x > 0}

step: P implies R1
rule: CONSEQUENCE
focus: P, R1
text:
  $R1$ is the weakest precondition of the program for $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R1$.
  >>> «P» ==> «R1»
vc: x > 5 ==> x > 0
why:
  Every integer greater than $5$ is greater than $0$.
instance: CONSEQUENCE
  x > 5 ==> x > 0
  {x > 0} skip {x > 0}
  ---
  {x > 5} skip {x > 0}
result: The triple is valid.

summary:
  The Skip rule only gives triples with the same precondition and
  postcondition. The consequence rule lets us use the stronger
  precondition $x > 5$. In general, $\{P\}$ `skip` $\{Q\}$ is valid exactly
  when $P ==> Q$.
